  :tonOfRefrigeration rdfs:comment "The ton of refrigeration is a unit of power defined as 3.516853e3 watt."@en .
  
  :RadiantFlux rdfs:comment "Radiant flux is the measure of the total power of electromagnetic radiation."@en .
  
  :millisecond-AnglePerYear rdfs:comment "Wordt gebruikt om de waargenomen verandering van de positie van sterren uit te drukken (de proper motion)."@nl .
  
  :Radiance rdfs:comment "Radiance is a radiometric measure that describes the amount of light that passes through or is emitted from a particular area and falls within a given solid angle in a specified direction."@en .
  
  :rhe rdfs:comment "The rhe is a unit of fluidity defined as 10 reciprocal pascal second (time)."@en .
  
  :Detectivity rdfs:comment "Reciprocal of Noise equivalent power. The signal-to-noise ratio for incident radiation of unit intensity."@en .
  
  :gramPerJoule rdfs:comment "Gram per joule is a unit of mass per energy."@en .
  
  :kilogramPerGigajoule rdfs:comment "Kilogram per gigajoule is a unit of mass per energy."@en .
  
  :Temperature rdfs:comment "Temperature is the extent to which an object is hot."@en .
  
  :Heat rdfs:comment "Heat is any flow of energy from one body or system to another due to a difference in temperature."@en .
  
  :InternalEnergy rdfs:comment "The internal energy of a thermodynamic system, or a body with well-defined boundaries is the total of the kinetic energy due to the motion of molecules (translational, rotational, vibrational) and the potential energy associated with the vibrational and electric energy of atoms within molecules or crystals."@en .
  
  :Entropy rdfs:comment "Entropy is a measure of the unavailability of a system’s energy to do work."@en .
  
  :ThermalConductivity rdfs:comment "Termal conductivity indicates the ability of a material to conduct heat."@en .
  
  :degreeFahrenheit rdfs:comment "The degree Fahrenheit is a unit of temperature defined as 5.555556e-1 kelvin."@en .
  
  :degreeRankine rdfs:comment "The degree Rankine is a unit of temperature defined as 5.555556e-1 kelvin."@en .
  
  :degreeReaumur rdfs:comment "The degree Réaumur is a unit of temperature defined as 1.25 kelvin."@en .
  
  :_-270.15To-268.15OnTheCelsiusScale rdfs:comment "One of the fixed points defining the Celsius scale"@en .
  
  :ElectromotiveForce rdfs:comment "Electromotive force is that which causes a flow of current."@en .
  
  :ElectricField rdfs:comment "Electric field is a property of the space surrounding an electric charge or in the presence of a time-varying magnetic field which exerts a forceon other electrically charged objects."@en .
  
  :ElectricChargeDensity rdfs:comment "Electric charge density is the amount of electric charge in a volume. It is a derived quantity in the International System of Units. Electric charge density is electric charge divided by volume."@en .
  
  :Permeance-Electromagnetic rdfs:comment "Electromagnetic permeance is a measure of flux for a number of current-turns in magnetic circuit."@en .
  
  :ElectricDipoleMoment rdfs:comment "Electric dipole moment is a measure of the polarity of a system of electric charges."@en .
  
  :Admittance rdfs:comment "Admittance is a measure of how easily a circuit or device will allow a current to flow. It is electric current divided by electric potential."@en .
  
  :faraday rdfs:comment "The faraday is a unit of electric charge defined as 9.648531e4 coulomb."@en .
  
  :mho rdfs:comment "The mho is a unit of electrical conductance defined as 1 siemens."@en .
  
  :unitPole rdfs:comment "The unit pole is a unit of magnetic flux defined as 1.256637e-7 weber."@en .
  
  :gamma rdfs:comment "The gamma is a unit of magnetic flux density defined as 1.0e-9 tesla."@en .
  
  :milligauss rdfs:comment "The milligauss is a unit of magnetic flux density defined as 1.0e-3 gauss."@en .
  
  :debye rdfs:comment "The debye is a unit of electric dipole moment defined as 3.33564e-30 coulomb metre."@en .
  
  :CompressiveStress rdfs:comment "Compressive stress is a stress that, when applied, acts towards the center of a material."@en .
  
  :ShearStress rdfs:comment "Shear stress is a stress that is applied parallel or tangential to a face of a material."@en .
  
  :ShearStrain rdfs:comment "Shear strain is a strain that acts parallel to the surface of a material that it acts on."@en .
  
  :ShearModulus rdfs:comment "Shear modulus is the ratio of shear stress to shear strain."@en .
  
  :BulkModulus rdfs:comment "Bulk modulus is a substance's resistance to uniform compression."@en .
  
  :EulerNumber rdfs:comment "The Euler number is a dimensionless number that expresses the relationship between a local pressure drop e.g. over a restriction and the kinetic energy per unit volume."@en .
  
  :FourierNumber rdfs:comment "The Fourier number is a dimensionless number that characterises heat conduction. It is the ratio of heat conduction rate to rate of thermal energy storage. The Fourier number is a dimensionless time."@en .
  
  :FroudeNumber rdfs:comment "The Froude number is a dimensionless number that compares inertial and gravitational forces. It may be used to quantify the resistance of an object moving through water, and compare objects of different sizes."@en .
  
  :GrashofNumber rdfs:comment "The Grashof number is a dimensionless number that approximates the ratio of buoyancy to viscous force that acts on a fluid."@en .
  
  :KnudsenNumber rdfs:comment "The Knudsen number is a dimensionless number defined as the ratio of the molecular mean free path length to a representative physical length scale."@en .
  
  :LewisNumber rdfs:comment "The Lewis number is a dimensionless number defined as the ratio of thermal diffusivity to mass diffusivity."@en .
  
  :MachNumber rdfs:comment "The Mach number is the speed of an object that moves through air, or any fluid substance, divided by the speed of sound as it is in that substance."@en .
  
  :NusseltNumber rdfs:comment "The Nusselt number is the ratio of convective to conductive heat transfer across (normal to) the boundary."@en .
  
  :PecletNumber rdfs:comment "The Péclet number is a dimensionless number that relates the rate of advection of a flow to its rate of diffusion, often thermal diffusion."@en .
  
  :RayleighNumber rdfs:comment "The Rayleigh number for a fluid is a dimensionless number associated with buoyancy driven flow."@en .
  
  :ReynoldsNumber rdfs:comment "The Reynolds number is a dimensionless number that gives a measure of the ratio of inertial forces to viscous forces and, consequently, quantifies the relative importance of these two types of forces for given flow conditions."@en .
  
  :StantonNumber rdfs:comment "The Stanton number is a dimensionless number that measures the ratio of heat transferred into a fluid to the thermal capacity of fluid."@en .
  
  :StrouhalNumber rdfs:comment "The Strouhal number is a dimensionless number that describes oscillating flow mechanisms."@en .
  
  :Molality rdfs:comment "Molality is the number of moles of solute per kilogram of solvent."@en .
  
  :molePerLitre rdfs:comment "Mole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :attomolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Attomole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as attomole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :centimolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Centimole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as centimole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :decamolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Decamole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as decamole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :decimolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Decimole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as decimole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :examolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Examole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as examole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :femtomolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Femtomole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as femtomole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :gigamolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Gigamole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as gigamole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :hectomolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Hectomole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as hectomole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :kilomolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Kilomole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as kilomole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :megamolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Megamole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as megamole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :micromolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Micromole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as micromole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :millimolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Millimole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as millimole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :nanomolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Nanomole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as nanomole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :petamolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Petamole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as petamole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :picomolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Picomole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as picomole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :teramolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Teramole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as teramole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :molePerAttolitre rdfs:comment "Mole per attolitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by attolitre."@en .
  
  :molePerCentilitre rdfs:comment "Mole per centilitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by centilitre."@en .
  
  :molePerDecalitre rdfs:comment "Mole per decalitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by decalitre."@en .
  
  :molePerDecilitre rdfs:comment "Mole per decilitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by decilitre."@en .
  
  :molePerExalitre rdfs:comment "Mole per exalitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by exalitre."@en .
  
  :molePerFemtolitre rdfs:comment "Mole per femtolitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by femtolitre."@en .
  
  :molePerGigalitre rdfs:comment "Mole per gigalitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by gigalitre."@en .
  
  :molePerHectolitre rdfs:comment "Mole per hectolitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by hectolitre."@en .
  
  :molePerKilolitre rdfs:comment "Mole per kilolitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by kilolitre."@en .
  
  :molePerMegalitre rdfs:comment "Mole per megalitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by megalitre."@en .
  
  :molePerMicrolitre rdfs:comment "Mole per microlitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by microlitre."@en .
  
  :molePerMillilitre rdfs:comment "Mole per millilitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by millilitre."@en .
  
  :molePerNanolitre rdfs:comment "Mole per nanolitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by nanolitre."@en .
  
  :molePerPetalitre rdfs:comment "Mole per petalitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by petalitre."@en .
  
  :molePerPicolitre rdfs:comment "Mole per picolitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by picolitre."@en .
  
  :molePerTeralitre rdfs:comment "Mole per teralitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by teralitre."@en .
  
  :molar rdfs:comment "Molar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1 mole per litre."@en .
  
  :attomolar rdfs:comment "The attomolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e-18 molar."@en .
  
  :femtomolar rdfs:comment "The femtomolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e-15 molar."@en .
  
  :megamolar rdfs:comment "The megamolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e6 molar."@en .
  
  :micromolar rdfs:comment "The micromolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e-6 molar."@en .
  
  :millimolar rdfs:comment "The millimolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e-3 molar."@en .
  
  :nanomolar rdfs:comment "The nanomolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e-9 molar."@en .
  
  :picomolar rdfs:comment "The picomolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e-12 molar."@en .
  
  :yoctomolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Yoctomole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as yoctomole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :zeptomolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Zeptomole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as zeptomole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :zettamolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Zettamole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as zettamole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :yottamolePerLitre rdfs:comment "Yottamole per litre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as yottamole divided by litre."@en .
  
  :molePerYoctolitre rdfs:comment "Mole per yoctolitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by yoctolitre."@en .
  
  :molePerZeptolitre rdfs:comment "Mole per zeptolitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by zeptolitre."@en .
  
  :molePerZettalitre rdfs:comment "Mole per zettalitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by zettalitre."@en .
  
  :molePerYottalitre rdfs:comment "Mole per yottalitre is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as mole divided by yottalitre."@en .
  
  :yoctomolar rdfs:comment "The yoctomolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e-24 molar."@en .
  
  :zeptomolar rdfs:comment "The zeptmolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e-21 molar."@en .
  
  :centimolar rdfs:comment "The centimolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e-2 molar."@en .
  
  :decimolar rdfs:comment "The decimolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e-1 molar."@en .
  
  :decamolar rdfs:comment "The decamolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e1 molar."@en .
  
  :hectomolar rdfs:comment "The hectomolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e2 molar."@en .
  
  :kilomolar rdfs:comment "The kilomolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e3 molar."@en .
  
  :gigamolar rdfs:comment "The gigamolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e9 molar."@en .
  
  :teramolar rdfs:comment "The teramolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e12 molar."@en .
  
  :petamolar rdfs:comment "The petamolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e15 molar."@en .
  
  :examolar rdfs:comment "The examolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e18 molar."@en .
  
  :zettamolar rdfs:comment "The zettamolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e21 molar."@en .
  
  :yottamolar rdfs:comment "The yottamolar is a unit of amount of substance concentration defined as 1.0e24 molar."@en .
  
  :CollisionFrequency rdfs:comment "Collision frequency is the average number of collisions between reacting molecules per unit time."@en .
  
  :amylaseUnit rdfs:comment "The amylase unit is a unit of catalytic activity."@en .
  
  :footlambert rdfs:comment "The footlambert is a unit of luminance defined as 3.426259 candela per square metre."@en .
  
  :lambert rdfs:comment "The lambert is a unit of luminance defined as 3.183099e3 candela per square metre."@en .
  
  :footcandle rdfs:comment "The footcandle is a unit of illuminance defined as 1.076391e1 lux."@en .
  
  :rad rdfs:comment "The rad is a unit of absorbed dose defined as 1.0e-2 gray."@en .
  
  :rem rdfs:comment "The rem is a unit of dose equivalent defined as 1.0e-2 sievert."@en .
  
  :curie rdfs:comment "The curie is a unit of activity defined as 3.7e10 becquerel."@en .
  
  :röntgen rdfs:comment "The röntgen is a unit of exposure to x and γ rays defined as 2.58e-4 coulomb per kilogram."@en .
  
  :Aberration rdfs:comment "The apparent angular displacement of the observed position of a celestial object from its geometric position, caused by the finite velocity of light in combination with the motions of the observer and of the observed object."@en .
  
  :AberrationInLatitude rdfs:comment "The apparent angular displacement in ecliptical latitude of the observed position of a celestial object from its geometric position, caused by the finite velocity of light in combination with the motions of the observer and of the observed object."@en .
  
  :AberrationInLongitude rdfs:comment "The apparent angular displacement in ecliptical longitude of the observed position of a celestial object from its geometric position, caused by the finite velocity of light in combination with the motions of the observer and of the observed object."@en .
  
  :AbsoluteMagnitude rdfs:comment "Logarithmic measure of the brightness of an object as seen from a standard distance of 10 pc. Units usually not indicated (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnitude_(astronomy)."@en .
  
  :AbsoluteBolometricMagnitude rdfs:comment "The absolute magnitude (see absolute magnitude) of a star is a measure of its total energy emission per second, or luminosity, i.e., the bolometric magnitude from a standard distance (10 pc)."@en .
  
  :Albedo rdfs:comment "Ratio between radiation falling onto an object and the radiation reflected or scattered back. Or the ratio between the illumination and observed brightness."@en .
  
  :Altitude rdfs:comment "The angular distance of a celestial body above or below the horizon, measured along the great circle passing through the body and the zenith."@en .
  
  :Amplitude rdfs:comment "The difference between the maximum and minimum magnitudes of a variable star, i.e., the total range of its brightness."@en .
  
  :VAmplitude rdfs:comment "Amplitude of the light variation in Johnson V magnitude. The Johnson V band is a standard passband in the visual area, matching the response curve of the human eye. The central wavelength is 550nm and the bandwidth is 90nm.  The filter to be used is the Corning 3384 filter."@en .
  
  :PhotographicAmplitude rdfs:comment "Amplitude of the light variation in photographic magnitude."@en .
  
  :AnnualAberration rdfs:comment "The component of the stellar abberation resulting from the motion of the Earth about the Sun. The abberation is the apparent angular displacement of the observed position of a celestial object from its geometric position, caused by the finite velocity of light in combination with the motions of the observer and of the observed object."@en .
  
  :ApparentDiameter rdfs:comment "The angle that the actual diameter of an object makes in the sky; also known as angular size. Most often small, so units are mostly arcminutes, arcseconds, or even milli- or microarcseconds."@en .
  
  :ApparentMagnitude rdfs:comment "Logarithmic measure of the apparent brightness of an object. Units usually not indicated(http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnitude_(astronomy)."@en .
  
  :Azimuth rdfs:comment "The angular distance measured clockwise along the horizon from a specified reference point (usually north) to the intersection with the great circle drawn from the zenith through a body on the celestial sphere."@en .
  
  :BMagnitude rdfs:comment "Johnson B magnitude. The Johnson B band is a standard filter in the blue area. The central wavelength is 440nm and the bandwidth is 100nm.  The filter to be used is the Corning 5030 filter plus the Schott GG13 filter."@en .
  
  :BMagnitudeAtMaximumBrightness rdfs:comment "Johnson B magnitude at maximum brightness (i.e. for a variable star). The Johnson B band is a standard filter in the blue area. The central wavelength is 440nm and the bandwidth is 100nm.  The filter to be used is the Corning 5030 filter plus the Schott GG13 filter."@en .
  
  :BMagnitudeAtMinimumBrightness rdfs:comment "Johnson B magnitude at minimum brightness (i.e. for a variable star). The Johnson B band is a standard filter in the blue area. The central wavelength is 440nm and the bandwidth is 100nm.  The filter to be used is the Corning 5030 filter plus the Schott GG13 filter."@en .
  
  :BolometricCorrection rdfs:comment "The visual magnitude of an object minus its bolometric magnitude."@en .
  
  :BolometricMagnitude rdfs:comment "The magnitude of a star measured across all wavelengths, so that it takes into account the total amount of energy radiated. If a star is a strong infrared or ultraviolet emitter, its bolometric magnitude  will differ greatly from its visual magnitude."@en .
  
  :BondAlbedo rdfs:comment "Is the fraction of the total incident solar radiation - the radiation at all wavelengths - that is reflected or scattered by an object in all directions."@en .
  
  :BrightnessTemperature rdfs:comment "The temperature that a blackbody would need to have in order to emit radiation of the observed intensity at a given wavelength (mostly used in radio astronomy)."@en .
  
  :Co-RotationRadius rdfs:comment "The radius (distance from the galaxy's centre) at which the stars move at the same speed as the spiral pattern or bar in a galaxy."@en .
  
  :ColdGasMassFraction rdfs:comment "The fraction of the mass of a galaxy that is in the form of cold gas ~10s K."@en .
  
  :ColourIndex rdfs:comment "The difference between the apparent magnitude of a star at two different wavelengths (always the shorter-wavelength magnitude minus the longer-wavelength magnitude) to give a quantification of the star's colour. The magnitude of an object at different wavelengths are measured by using different filters before the detector. Often the Johnson system with UBV passbands are used. Other passbands may also be used (for instance g-r)."@en .
  
  :ColourTemperature rdfs:comment "The temperature of a blackbody that has the same colour index as a given star."@en .
  
  :Cut-OffWavelength rdfs:comment "Either: wavelengths at which the detectivity (D) falls to 0, or the wavelengths at which the detectivity falls to 1% of the peak value, or the wavelengths at which the normalised detectivity (D*) has fallen to half its peak value."@en .
  
  :DarkNoise rdfs:comment "Output from a detector when unilluminated - usually as RMS voltage or current (Kitchin, Astrophysical Techniques, IoP, Table 1.1.2)."@en .
  
  :Declination rdfs:comment "The angular distance on the celestial sphere north or south of the celestial equator. It is measured along the hour circle passing through the celestial object. Declination is usually given in combination with right ascension or hour angle."@en .
  
  :DetectiveQuantumEfficiency rdfs:comment "Square of ratio between the output signal noise ratio and the input signal noise ratio."@en .
  
  :DistanceModulus rdfs:comment """The difference between the apparent magnitude (m) of an astronomical object, such as a star, and its absolute magnitude (M), used as a distance measurement. Distances can be expressed in distance modulii as $$m-M = 5\\log d + 10 = 10-5 log \\varpi$$ where \\(d\\) is the distance in kiloparsec and \\(\\varpi\\) is the parallax in milliarcseconds.
    """@en .
  
  :DiurnalAberration rdfs:comment "The component of the stellar abberation resulting from the diurnal motion of the observer around the centre of the Earth. The abberation is the apparent angular displacement of the observed position of a celestial object from its geometric position, caused by the finite velocity of light in combination with the motions of the observer and of the observed object."@en .
  
  :DynamicRange rdfs:comment "Ratio between the saturation output and the dark signal, sometimes only over the region of linearity."@en .
  
  :Eccentricity rdfs:comment "A measure of the deviation from a circle for an orbit."@en .
  
  :EclipticLatitude rdfs:comment "The angular distance on the celestial sphere north or south of the ecliptic (the path of the Sun on the celestial sphere during one year). It is measured along the great circle passing through the object and the ecliptic poles and perpendicular to the ecliptic."@en .
  
  :EclipticLongitude rdfs:comment "The angular distance on the celestial sphere measured clockwise from the vernal equinox along the ecliptic (the path of the Sun on the celestial sphere during one year) to the intersection with the great circle drawn from the ecliptical north pole through the object."@en .
  
  :ElectronTemperature rdfs:comment "The temperature determined by the mean kinetic energy of free electrons in a plasma; also known as kinetic temperature."@en .
  
  :Ellipticity rdfs:comment "A measure of the amount by which an object, such as a planet or a galaxy, deviates from a perfect sphere."@en .
  
  :Extinction rdfs:comment "Total extinction at a specific wavelength. The extinction is caused by dust and gas between a star and the observer. It is the difference between the observed magnitude and the magnitude the source would have had if no extinction had taken place."@en .
  
  :GalacticCylindricalPolarAngleCoordinate rdfs:comment "The angle from the Galactic centre between the perpendicular projection of the Sun on the Galactic plane and the projection of the object. This is one of the three Galactic Cylindrical Polar Coordinates."@en .
  
  :GalacticLatitude rdfs:comment "The angular distance on the celestial sphere north or south of the galactic equator. It is measured along the great circle passing through the object and the galactic poles and perpendicular to the galactic equator."@en .
  
  :GalacticLongitude rdfs:comment "The angular distance on the celestial sphere measured clockwise from the galactic centre (as defined by the International Astronomical Union (IAU)) along the galactic equator to the intersection with the great circle drawn from the galactic north pole through the object."@en .
  
  :GeometricalAlbedo rdfs:comment "Ratio between the brightness of an object as seen from the direction of a hypothetical white, diffusely reflecting sphere of the same size and at the same distance."@en .
  
  :HourAngle rdfs:comment "The angular distance on the celestial sphere measured westward along the celestial equator from the meridian to the hour circle that passes through the celestial object."@en .
  
  :InitialMassFunction rdfs:comment "The number of stars in mass fraction dM around mass M. Used in Salpeter's Initial Mass Function (IMF)."@en .
  
  :IntegratedMagnitude rdfs:comment "The apparent magnitude that an extended object, such as a nebula or galaxy, would have if all its light were concentrated at a starlike point."@en .
  
  :IntrinsicColourIndex rdfs:comment "The colour index a star would have in the absence of interstellar extinction (reddening). It is assumed that all stars of the same spectral type and luminosity class have the same colour index."@en .
  
  :IonizationTemperature rdfs:comment "The temperature of a gas or plasma derived from the relative numbers of neutral atoms and ions. Specifically, it is the temperature for which the Saha equations would predict these relative numbers, assuming the atoms and ions are in thermodynamic equilibrium."@en .
  
  :JeansMass rdfs:comment "The critical mass of a molecular cloud, above which it will be unstable to collapse."@en .
  
  :LightTime rdfs:comment "The time electromagnetic radiation takes to reach Earth from a distant source. Often the correction in light time is needed to accurately calculate the apparent position of solar system objects or to calculate the period of variable stars (different times are observed when the Earth is at a different position in its orbit)."@en .
  
  :LimitingMagnitude rdfs:comment "The magnitude of the faintest object (star) that can be detected by a telescope or other instrument. Depends not only on the telescope but also on the detector and on the observing method."@en .
  
  :LuminosityFunction rdfs:comment "The number of stars of absolute magnitudes between Mv and Mv+ΔMv per cubic parsec."@en .
  
  :EpochAtMaximumBrightness rdfs:comment "A moment when the object (i.e. a variable star) was at maximum brightness."@en .
  
  :Magnitude rdfs:comment "Reverse logarithmic measure of the brightness of an object."@en .
  
  :MagnitudeAtMaximumBrightness rdfs:comment "The magnitude at maximum brightness of a variable star."@en .
  
  :MagnitudeAtMinimumBrightness rdfs:comment "The magnitude at minimum brightness of a variable star."@en .
  
  :Metallicity rdfs:comment "The log of the ratio between the ratios of the observed Fe and H quantities in a star and the same ratio in the Sun. This is a very important quantity that is often used in astronomy as an indicator of the age of a star."@en .
  
  :NoiseEquivalentPower rdfs:comment "Radiative flux on a detector needed for a signal/noise ratio of 1 (Kitchin, Astrophysical Techniques, IoP, Table 1.1.2)."@en .
  
  :NormalAlbedo rdfs:comment "Ratio between radiation falling vertically onto an object and the radiation radiated back vertically."@en .
  
  :NormalisedDetectivity rdfs:comment "The detectivity normalised by multiplying by the square root of the detector area, and by the electrical bandwidth. The units cm Hz(1/2)/W are commonly used and it then represents the signal-to-noise ratio when 1 W of radiation is incident on a detector with an area of 1 cm2, and the electrical bandwidth is 1 Hz."@en .
  
  :PeakWavelength rdfs:comment "Wavelength for which the detectivity is at a maximum."@en .
  
  :PlanetaryAberration rdfs:comment "The apparent angular displacement of the observed position of a celestial object produced by the motion of the observer and the actual motion of the observed object."@en .
  
  :QuantumEfficiency rdfs:comment "Ratio (of a detector such as a CCD) of actual number of detected photons and the number of incident photons."@en .
  
  :RedMagnitude rdfs:comment "A red magnitude not specified for a specific photometric system."@en .
  
  :Reddening rdfs:comment "Reddening causes the star to appear redder if more dust or gas is between the star and the observer."@en .
  
  :ReddeningB-V rdfs:comment "Reddening causes the star to appear redder if more dust or gas is between the star and the observer. The standard reddening is measured using the B and V passbands."@en .
  
  :ReddeningU-B rdfs:comment "Reddening measured with the U and B passbands."@en .
  
  :Responsivity rdfs:comment "Detector output for unit intensity input. Units are usually volts per watt or amps per watt."@en .
  
  :RightAscension rdfs:comment "The angular distance on the celestial sphere measured eastward along the celestial equator from the equinox to the great circle passing through the celestial object and the celestial north pole."@en .
  
  :ScaleHeight rdfs:comment "The scale height of a feature (such as the thin galactic disk) is the height (or position) at which the number density of the feature (for instance of the number of Population II stars) is equal to 1/e times the number density at the origin (for instance the Galactic Plane)."@en .
  
  :ScaleLength rdfs:comment "The radial distance from a galaxy's core at which the average intensity has fallen to 1/e of the intensity at the centre of the galaxy."@en .
  
  :SecularAberration rdfs:comment "The component of the stellar abberation resulting from the motion of the solar system in space. This component is usually ignored. The abberation is the apparent angular displacement of the observed position of a celestial object from its geometric position, caused by the finite velocity of light in combination with the motions of the observer and of the observed object."@en .
  
  :SpectralResponse rdfs:comment "The change in output signal as a function of changes in the wavelength of the input signal."@en .
  
  :StellarAberration rdfs:comment "The apparent angular displacement of the observed position of a celestial object resulting from the motion of the observer. Stellar aberration is divided into diurnal, annual, and secular components."@en .
  
  :SupergalacticLatitude rdfs:comment "The angular distance on the celestial sphere north or south of the supergalactic equator. It is measured along the great circle passing through the object and the supergalactic poles and perpendicular to the supergalactic equator."@en .
  
  :SupergalacticLongitude rdfs:comment "The angular distance on the celestial sphere measured clockwise from the supergalactic centre (as defined by the International Astronomical Union (IAU)) along the supergalactic equator to the intersection with the great circle drawn from the supergalactic north pole through the object."@en .
  
  :TimeConstant rdfs:comment "Time required to approach (1-1/e) of the final output value of a detector (about 63%) (Kitchin, Astrophysical Techniques, IoP, Table 1.1.2)."@en .
  
  :TychoBroadbandMagnitude rdfs:comment "Broadband Tycho magnitude (formed from B and V magintude measurements."@en .
  
  :JohnsonMagnitude rdfs:comment "A magnitude measured in one of Johnson's standard passbands (using a standard filter, i.e. U, B, or V)."@en .
  
  :StroemgrenMagnitude rdfs:comment "A magnitude measured in one of Strömgren's standard passbands (using a standard filter, u, b, v, or y) or in the passbands defined by Crawford (β_narrow or β_wide)."@en .
  
  :CousinsMagnitude rdfs:comment "A magnitude measured in one of Cousins standard passbands (using a standard filter, i.e. I or R)."@en .
  
  :ThuanAndGunnMagnitude rdfs:comment "A magnitude measured in one of Thuan and Gunn's standard passbands (using a standard filter, i.e. g)."@en .
  
  :UMagnitude rdfs:comment "Johnson U magnitude. The Johnson U band is a standard passband in the ultraviolet area. The central wavelength is 365nm and the bandwidth is 70nm.  The filter to be used is the Corning 9863 filter."@en .
  
  :VMagnitude rdfs:comment "Johnson V magnitude. The Johnson V band is a standard passband in the visual area, matching the response curve of the human eye. The central wavelength is 550nm and the bandwidth is 90nm.  The filter to be used is the Corning 3384 filter."@en .
  
  :VMagnitudeAtMaximumBrightness rdfs:comment "Johnson V magnitude (apparent) at maximum brightness (i.e. for a variable star). The Johnson V band is a standard filter in the visual area, matching the response curve of the human eye. The central wavelength is 550nm and the bandwidth is 90nm.  The filter to be used is the Corning 3384 filter."@en .
  
  :VMagnitudeAtMinimumBrightness rdfs:comment "Johnson V magnitude (apparent) at minimum brightness (i.e. for a variable star). The Johnson V band is a standard filter in the visual area, matching the response curve of the human eye. The central wavelength is 550nm and the bandwidth is 90nm.  The filter to be used is the Corning 3384 filter."@en .
  
  :VisualAlbedo rdfs:comment "The albedo only for radiation in the visual part of the spectrum."@en .
  
  :ZenithDistance rdfs:comment "The angular distance on the celestial sphere measured along the great circle from the zenith to the celestial object. z = 90° - h."@en .
  
  :_1040NanometreLockwoodMagnitude rdfs:comment "A magnitude in the 1.04 micrometre band of the photometric system introduced by G.W. Lockwood."@en .
  
  :uMagnitude rdfs:comment "u Magnitude in the Strömgren photometric system with a peak wavelength at 350 nm and a peak-half-width of 30 nm."@en .
  
  :vMagnitude rdfs:comment "v Magnitude in the Strömgren photometric system with a peak wavelength at 411 nm and a peak-half-width of 19 nm."@en .
  
  :bMagnitude rdfs:comment "b Magnitude in the Strömgren photometric system with a peak wavelength at 467 nm and a peak-half-width of 18 nm."@en .
  
  :yMagnitude rdfs:comment "y Magnitude in the Strömgren photometric system with a peak wavelength at 547 nm and a peak-half-width of 23 nm."@en .
  
  :BetaNarrowMagnitude rdfs:comment "β_narrow  Magnitude in the Strömgren-Crawford photometric system with a peak wavelength at 485.8 nm and a peak-half-width of 2.9 nm."@en .
  
  :BetaWideMagnitude rdfs:comment "β_wide  Magnitude in the Strömgren-Crawford photometric system with a peak wavelength at 485 nm and a peak-half-width of 12.9 nm."@en .
  
  :IMagnitude rdfs:comment "I magnitude in the Cousins photometric system."@en .
  
  :RMagnitude rdfs:comment "R magnitude in the Cousins photometric system."@en .
  
  :gMagnitude rdfs:comment "g Magnitude in the Thuan and Gunn photometric system."@en .
  
  :hour-HourAngle rdfs:comment "Wordt gebruikt als hoek (360°=24) voor o.a. rechte klimming. De h wordt meestal als superscript achter de waarde gezet gevolgd door de verdere opdeling naar minuten en seconden. Zoals in 5h34m12s09. Vaak wordt de fractie in seconden zonder punt geschreven, de s wordt als afscheiding gebruikt (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_ascension)."@nl .
  
  :kilometrePerSecond-TimePerMegaparsec rdfs:comment "De eenheid van de Hubble constante (die niet constant is!) (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hubble_constant)."@en .
  
  :magnitude rdfs:comment "Eenheid waarmee de helderheid van sterren wordt aangegeven. Meestal wordt het symbool niet aangeduid (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnitude_(astronomy))."@en .
  
  :magnitudePerSecond-AngleSquared rdfs:comment "The brightness (in magnitudes) of an area on the celestial sphere of 1 arcsecond by 1 arcsecond."@en .
  
  :micromagnitude rdfs:comment "The micromagnitude is a unit of magnitude defined as 1.0e-6 magnitude."@en .
  
  :millimagnitude rdfs:comment "The millimagnitude is a unit of magnitude defined as 1.0e-3 magnitude."@en .
  
  :minute-HourAngle rdfs:comment "Wordt gebruikt als hoek (360°=1440) voor o.a. rechte klimming. De m wordt meestal als superscript achter de waarde gezet gevolgd door de verdere opdeling naar seconden. Zoals in 5h34m12s09. Vaak wordt de fractie in seconden zonder punt geschreven, de s wordt als afscheiding gebruikt (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_ascension)."@nl .
  
  :second-HourAngle rdfs:comment "Wordt gebruikt als hoek (360°=864000) voor o.a. rechte klimming. De s wordt meestal als superscript achter de waarde gezet. Zoals in 5h34m12s09. Vaak wordt de fractie in seconden zonder punt geschreven, de s wordt als afscheiding gebruikt (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_ascension)."@nl .
  
  :solarMassPerGigayearCubicKiloparsec rdfs:comment "The amount of stellar mass created per cubic kiloparsec in each billion years."@en .
  
  :solarMassPerGigayearCubicParsec rdfs:comment "The amount of stellar mass created per cubic parsec in each billion years."@en .
  
  :wattPerSecond-AngleSquared rdfs:comment "The radiative intensity (in watts) of an area on the celestial sphere of 1 arcsecond by 1 arcsecond."@en .
  
